#!/usr/bin/env python3
# autoharn-verb-desc: write to or read from the append-only governance ledger (decisions, work items, obligations, principals, ...)
"""led -- read from and write to this project's decision ledger.

usage: led <kind> <statement...> [flags]        write an entry (decision, finding, assumption, ...)
       led --recent [N] | led current [N]        [--kind <kind>] [--fields f1,f2,...]
       led show <id>                             [--fields f1,f2,...]
       led question-status | review-gap | stamp-distinctness | standing
           review-gap (bare, led-review-gap-false-clean, rows 1087/1102): reads BOTH the
           actor-keyed /views/review_gap AND item-keyed /views/work_review_gap, each row labeled
           gap_kind ("actor" | "work_item") -- never silently empty while work-item debt exists.
           Every review-gap row (bare or `led work review-gap`) also carries review_status
           ("never-reviewed" | "reviewed-not-discharging", the latter with reviewing_verdicts
           naming who/what/independence) -- refuse-verdict-legibility (this brief item 3), built
           from the already-served /views/review_verdicts, CLI-presentation layer only.
       led register-principal <name> <class> [--purpose "<why>"]
       led obligate <scope> <assigned-by> <obliged-actor>
       led obligate revoke <scope> --reason "<text>"
       led review <entry-id> <verdict> <independence> <statement...>
       led work open|claim|depends|close|list|violations|asof|review-gap|startable|
           resolve-violation|supersede-cascade|reclose ...   (see `led work <sub-verb>` with no
           args)
           led work list [--all] [--state open|closed] [--slug <slug>] [--fields f1,f2,...]
           led work reclose <slug> <resolution> [close-contract flags...] (B2, rows 1025/1008/
           1200): supersedes <slug>'s current close row (s31) and issues the corrected close in
           one act -- the SAME flags `led work close` accepts, including `--review-annulled
           <ref>` (s73's typed annulment disposition; authored, not yet live on every world --
           the kernel's own refusal, where it applies, is definitive).
       led artifact put <path> | get <hash> | stat <hash>
       led principal <sub-verb> ...                  (see `led principal` with no args)
       led missive list
       led missive send --thread <minting-world>/<slug> --act <assertion|request|response|
           withdrawal> --addressee <world> --statement "<text>" [--responds-to <local-receipt-
           row-id>|xrow:<world>:<id>:<row_hash>]
           seq is resolved automatically off this world's own missive_outbound feed; author-
           world comes from this deployment, never a typed flag; 'acknowledgment' is not
           sendable here (minted only by 'led missive dispose').
       led missive dispose <receipt-row-id> <disposition> [statement...]
           disposition (kernel/lineage/s58-missive-substrate.sql's own closed vocabulary):
           consumed | declined | superseded-unread | escalated
       led decomposition-review-status | led briefing
       led --json <ledger|review|registration|obligation|obligation_revoke|missive_dispose>
           <file|->

Run any subcommand with no arguments, or a malformed one, for its own one-line usage. Every
entry is attributed to the actual connection identity, never a name you type; `led --recent`
and `led show <id>` read entries back.

led-read-projection-flags (this brief, item 1): `--recent`/`current`/`show`/`work list` accept
`--fields f1,f2,...` (JSON-per-line projection, unknown field name REFUSED, never a silent
empty), `--recent`/`current` accept `--kind <kind>` (REFUSED against the live `GET /kinds`
vocabulary), and `work list` accepts `--state open|closed` / `--slug <slug>` -- replacing the
ad-hoc `python3 -c` filters piping this CLI's own output that orchestrator sessions kept
reaching for.

This CLI talks HTTP to this project's boundary service rather than psql directly (the served
rebase, design/FABLE-BOUNDARY-MULTIPLEX-AND-CLI-REBASE-SPEC.md §5, ratified ledger row 1631).
Full coverage detail, transport/exit-code conventions, and every disclosed behavior difference
from the retired direct-psql tool (`./legacy/led`, now a one-line teaching-refusal stub) are
kept verbatim, not deleted (ADR-0020), in
design/LED-BOUNDARY-REBASE-COVERAGE.md -- nobody needs to read that file to use this tool.

TRANSPORT: every read is a GET against `/rows/*`/`/views/*`/`/standing/principals` (paginated,
walked to completion client-side, spec's own "no server-side filter grammar" discipline); every
write is a POST against `/write/<surface>`, the kernel's own `write_verdict` passed through
byte-verbatim (spec §5: "same typed verdicts"). Exit codes follow `boundary_cli_client.py`'s own
convention (module docstring there): 0 kernel-accepted, 1 kernel-refused (byte-identical to the
legacy tool's own exit for this case), 3 boundary-refused, 4 boundary-unreachable/client error.

Lazy imports banned (CLAUDE.md, 2026-07-02): every import is top-of-file."""
